  "result": {
    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.all_spanning_trees - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.all_spanning_trees.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.all_spanning_trees()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Enumerate all spanning trees of the graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.all_spanning_trees()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Enumerate all spanning trees of the graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.all_spanning_trees()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Enumerate all spanning trees of the graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nEnumerate all spanning trees of the graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L90018-L90172){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.all_spanning_trees(\n    max_trees=None,\n    weight_attr=None,\n    return_subgraphs=True,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![All spanning trees](../assets/graphs/all-spanning-trees.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: a triangle A, B, C with a pendant node D attached to C. Right: its three spanning trees, one per row; each keeps edge C–D and drops one triangle edge (drawn dashed), with the tree edges A–C B–C C–D, A–B B–C C–D, and A–B A–C C–D listed beside them.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA spanning tree is a subgraph that connects all vertices with the minimum number of edges\n(`V-1` edges for `V` vertices) and contains no cycles. Where `minimum_spanning_tree()`\nreturns one optimal tree, this method lists every distinct spanning tree (up to\n`max_trees=`), regardless of weight.\n\nEnumerating all spanning trees is useful for:\n\n- reliability analysis: each tree is a minimal set of links that keeps the network\n  connected, and the number of trees is a measure of redundancy\n- network design: comparing every possible backbone by a custom criterion (total weight,\n  diameter, maximum degree) that a single MST can't optimize\n- teaching and exploring the combinatorics of small graphs\n\nThe search works through the edges in a fixed order, building up acyclic edge sets and\nrecording each one that reaches `V - 1` edges. The number of trees grows extremely fast\nwith graph density, so this is only practical for small graphs; `max_trees=` caps the\noutput.\n\nEdges are treated as undirected for the cycle check. On undirected graphs, parallel edges\nbetween the same pair of nodes are collapsed (only the first is used) and self-loops are\nnever part of a tree. On directed graphs, the up-front connectivity check follows edge\ndirection from one starting node, so a graph that is only weakly connected may be\nrejected; use `to_undirected()` first in that case.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[max_trees]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Maximum number of spanning trees to return. If `None` (the default), at most `1000`\n    trees are returned to prevent excessive memory usage. Pass a larger value to see more\n    trees, or a smaller one to grab a quick sample. Enumeration stops as soon as this many\n    trees have been found.\n\n<code>[weight_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Name of the edge attribute used for the weights reported in the results (the weight\n    in each edge tuple, or the edge weight in each returned `Graph`). It does not affect\n    which trees are found. If `None` (the default), each edge's weight field is used (or\n    `1.0` if the edge has none), as it is for `\"weight\"`. Under any other name, every edge\n    must hold a number.\n\n<code>[return_subgraphs]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True` (the default), returns a list of `Graph` objects, each containing every node\n    of the original graph and the edges of one spanning tree. If `False`, returns a list\n    of edge lists, where each edge is a `(source_id, target_id, weight)` tuple; this\n    avoids building a `Graph` per tree and is much faster for large outputs.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list[Graph] or list[list[tuple[int, int, float]]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Either a list of Graph objects or a list of edge lists, depending on the\n    `return_subgraphs=` parameter. Each returned `Graph` has the same directedness and\n    weighting as the original and keeps every node and the tree's edges with their\n    original IDs, labels, weights and attributes. An empty or single-node graph yields\n    a single tree with no edges.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is disconnected (no spanning tree exists), or `weight_attr=` names an\n    attribute that an edge lacks or holds as a non-number.\n:::\n\n## Warnings {.doc-warnings}\n\nThe number of spanning trees can be extremely large. For a complete graph `Kn`, there are\n`n^(n-2)` spanning trees (Cayley's formula). Use `max_trees=` to limit results.\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nBy Cayley's formula (1889) the complete graph $K_n$ has $n^{n - 2}$ spanning trees,\nand that combinatorial explosion is not confined to complete graphs: even mildly\ndense inputs produce tree counts that exceed available memory well before\nenumeration finishes. The implementation is a depth-first backtracking search over\nedge subsets: edges are added in index order, any partial set containing a cycle\n(detected with union-find) is pruned, and every acyclic set of $V - 1$ edges is a\nspanning tree, so each distinct tree is produced exactly once. The running time\ngrows with the number of acyclic edge subsets explored, which can be far larger\nthan the number of trees returned. The `max_trees=` cap (defaulting to $1000$) is a\nhard budget, not a hint; treat it as the guardrail it is. Kirchhoff's matrix-tree\ntheorem lets you count spanning trees analytically as $\\det(L^*)$ (a cofactor of the\nLaplacian) when you only need the *number* and not the enumeration itself.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~iter_spanning_trees`) \n\n:   The spanning trees one at a time, in order of weight.\n\n[](`~number_of_spanning_trees`) \n\n:   Count them without listing them.\n\n[](`~random_spanning_tree`) \n\n:   Draw one of them at random.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA triangle has three spanning trees, one for each edge left out:\n\n::: {#c731a2a7 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ntriangle = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\")])\n\n[\n    t.get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)\n    for t in triangle.all_spanning_trees()\n]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n[['B--C', 'C--A'], ['A--B', 'C--A'], ['A--B', 'B--C']]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Every way to tie the families together\n\n`florentine_families()` links the leading families of Renaissance Florence by marriage.\nTaking 14 of its 20 marriages can tie the 15 families together in 1208 ways. In the full\nnetwork, no two families are more than five marriages apart. Most trees stretch that to\neight, and the best keep it to six:\n\n::: {#00d5b2ae .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nfamilies = ec.florentine_families()\ntrees = families.all_spanning_trees(max_trees=2000)\n\nspread = Counter(t.diameter() for t in trees)\nlen(trees), sorted(spread.items())\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(1208, [(6, 77), (7, 342), (8, 454), (9, 260), (10, 67), (11, 7), (12, 1)])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nOne of the 77 trees in which every family is within six marriages of every other:\n\n::: {#1a2f04df .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nbest = min(trees, key=lambda t: t.diameter())\nfamilies.plot(\n    highlight_edges=best.get_edge_ids(), label_by=\"label\", seed=2\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f9